Task must not take a motion status from before a command as current.

Motion acknowledges a command in motion-command-handler and acts on it
later, in motion-controller: it queues the move, enables the machine,
switches mode.  A status read in between, or a failed read that leaves
task with its copy from before the command, shows motion idle.  Task
then finishes the command too soon: wait_complete() returns before the
machine is on or in auto mode, and the line after a G38 move takes the
result of the probe before, or fails with "Queue is not empty after
probing" (#3650).

Both tests check that the machine is on and in auto mode once
wait_complete() returns, then probe at alternating heights and fail if
a probe reads the height of the one before.

late-controller runs motion-controller before motion-command-handler,
so a whole servo period passes between the two.  The period is 50 ms,
longer than the var file write task does when it synchs after a probe.

slow-controller uses kinematics that busy wait, so motion-controller
takes most of the servo period and status reads by task often fail
while it runs.
